ows FFmpeg is first verified against yt-dlp/FFmpeg-Builds and then redistributed as an immutable versioned asset of the app Release so the upstream moving `latest` URL cannot invalidate a published manifest.

## Architecture and Current Behavior

- The frontend creates each download UUID before invoking Rust and creates the popup state first. Rust uses that UUID for every event. A bounded frontend pending-event map protects against exceptionally early events.
- The single-download URL field performs only immediate local HTTP(S) syntax validation. It does not call oEmbed or yt-dlp, wait for metadata, or claim that a service is unavailable before the user starts. Actual extractor support, authorization, metadata, and friendly errors are reported by the download popup.
- `.ru`/`.рф` domains require the private VPN decision only after the user presses Download and before yt-dlp starts. Approval is cached only for the same normalized URL.
- URLs that visibly look like a collection, plus Threads post/share URLs that may resolve to a carousel, show an explicit `Лише це відео` / `Уся добірка` choice without a network probe. Single is the default and always sends `--no-playlist`; collection mode sends `--yes-playlist` and pacing options. Extractor-only collection metadata is handled during the actual download rather than delaying the main field.
- The preflight is an invisible, single-click, network-free safety step based on local disk data and any values already known at start. It immediately starts the download when space is sufficient. There is no preflight card or second confirmation click. Size/free-space details belong in the active job popup; the live disk guard remains authoritative.
- Starting a job clears the URL field. Successful completion keeps it empty; confirmed user cancellation restores the exact URL and playlist intent so the user can change the destination and retry immediately.
- The URL field has a dedicated one-click clear control. The sidebar version is read from `package.json.releaseVersion`, the deferred Settings item is hidden, and Download/History are real navigable views.
- The Download header communicates broad support in one concise line: video, audio, and subtitles from YouTube, Instagram, Twitter, and almost anywhere else; it deliberately avoids presenting a redundant service checker or publicly naming Russian platforms.
- Terminal jobs retain already-compatible final playlist outputs even when a later item is cancelled, hits low disk or needs authentication; no new conversion starts after such an interruption. History stores real outputs plus explicitly labelled failed/cancelled/auth attempts, without fabricating file paths. The shared 500-record limit, 20-report limit, per-file identity/retry and availability checks remain; pathless attempts are excluded from filesystem polling.
- History thumbnails are downloaded in the background only when a terminal job produced at least one real final output. They live in the app-local `history-thumbnails` directory, survive offline use, show a local placeholder if a cached file becomes unavailable, and are removed when History is cleared. Cache/write/clear/delete operations are serialized, and a late cache result cleans itself up when its history entry was cleared without deleting a thumbnail shared by another entry. The cache accepts only raster image MIME types, rejects files above 8 MiB, retains at most 550 files, rejects private/loopback/link-local/special IP ranges, pins DNS resolution for each request, revalidates every redirect, and is exposed through a narrowly scoped Tauri asset protocol.
- Persisted history is parsed through a strict runtime normalizer: invalid dates or missing identity/source fields are dropped; missing paths are accepted only for explicitly labelled failed/cancelled/auth attempts. Legacy file records retain bounded defaults for sizes, availability and retry settings. Availability polling reads current entries through a ref and preserves state identity when file status did not change.
- `Завантажити ще раз` restores the original URL, destination, video/audio mode, quality, audio format, subtitle flag, and single/collection intent. It falls back to the system Downloads directory if the old destination disappeared, runs the local preflight, and then starts automatically.
- Unknown estimates remain visibly unknown. On macOS, user-facing and preflight capacity uses Foundation's important-usage value so APFS purgeable space matches the system Storage UI; Windows and other targets use their normal available-space value. The live guard deliberately remains conservative and checks immediately writable blocks during yt-dlp, merge, video FFmpeg, and audio FFmpeg.
- The runtime guard triggers at 628 MiB free (500 MiB protected reserve plus 128 MiB write buffer), kills the active process group, preserves completed/user source files, and removes only the app-owned FFmpeg temporary output.
- Video downloads that require compatibility processing are merged to an intermediate file and converted by a managed FFmpeg process to 8-bit `yuv420p` H.264/AAC MP4 (hardware bitrate target; software CRF 18) with real percent, speed, and ETA. macOS probes VideoToolbox and falls back once to `libx264`. Windows treats the real conversion as the authoritative hardware test and tries NVENC, Quick Sync, and AMF in that order before `libx264`; this avoids false software selection caused by a short synthetic driver warm-up probe. The most recent successful hardware encoder is preferred until the managed FFmpeg executable changes; remaining hardware candidates still run after failure, and software fallback is not cached. A ready single-file H.264/AAC MP4 bypasses MKV, merge, and app-managed FFmpeg regardless of its website; a bounded managed-ffprobe stream check verifies pixel format and HDR as well as codecs, and its short-lived file-identity cache avoids probing the same unchanged file twice. `--merge-output-format mkv` remains enabled but yt-dlp ignores it when no merge is required; universal `--remux-video mkv` is deliberately absent.
- TikTok starts with yt-dlp's explicit `Chrome-99:Android-12` impersonation target and, after a 403, retries exactly once through yt-dlp's current desktop Chrome target. Real confirmed posts selectively require different profiles, and the desktop retry preserves user-selected browser cookies. URL canonicalization and the normal extractor remain unchanged. Threads continues to use the generic Chrome target. Safari is not offered for browser-cookie retry because macOS denies ordinary desktop apps access to `Cookies.binarycookies`; macOS defaults to Chrome and old persisted Safari selections are migrated away.
- Source-file ownership: yt-dlp reports an existing same-named file as if it had just saved it. A source is replaced or deleted after conversion only when its creation time is at or after the job start (`source_created_by_job`, `--no-mtime` keeps that time honest); unknown means keep. A job's own same-named source is replaced in place, so no needless ` (1)`.
- Sign-in detection reads only yt-dlp `ERROR:` lines, and a process that exits successfully can never end as failed or auth_required (`terminal_kind`). Status codes match whole numbers only (`errors.rs`), because titles, IDs and speeds such as `403.21KiB/s` contain the same digits.
- Quality limits are `-S res:N` preferences, not `[height<=N]` filters: `res` is the shorter frame side, so vertical video counts correctly, and a video available only larger or without size metadata still downloads.
- Managed FFmpeg/Deno are located by file existence for a download, never via PATH; a timed-out status probe cannot swap in a system binary. Same-codec audio (M4A/AAC, MP3, Opus) is stream-copied rather than re-encoded.
- Inside a playlist or carousel the progress template adds `playlist_autonumber|n_entries`, so the bar covers the whole collection with `Файл N із M`. Completion reports the saved-file count and offers `Показати у папці`.
- An output folder that disappears is a temporary state: the session uses Downloads with a visible notice, the saved choice stays in storage, and the folder returns when available. Whole-collection URLs (`/playlist` without `v`, `/sets/`, `/album/`...) show no misleading single-item choice and always use collection pacing.
- Code layout: `lib.rs` (download flow, commands, tests), `runtime.rs`, `runtime_check.rs`, `ytdlp_onedir.rs`, `ytdlp_signature.rs`, `errors.rs`, `conversion.rs`, `thumbnails.rs`, `vpn.rs`; frontend `App.tsx`, `history.ts`, `HistoryView.tsx`, `QueueViews.tsx`, `RuntimeViews.tsx`, `Icon.tsx`, `format.ts`.
- `.github/workflows/upstream-canary.yml` runs the Instagram plugin tests daily against current yt-dlp, because the app follows nightly by itself and the plugin overrides upstream internals.
- Instagram photos: the bundled `instagram_photos.py` (installed as `instagram_baldojnyi.py` next to the Threads/discovery modules) overrides yt-dlp's built-in InstagramIE through the official `plugin_name` mechanism and keeps `IE_NAME` `Instagram`. It calls the upstream extraction first and only for a post/carousel slide with neither `video_versions` nor a DASH manifest adds one format `baldojnyi-photo` (largest `image_versions2` candidate, `vcodec`/`acodec` `none`) plus `baldojnyi_photo: true`; video results are returned untouched and any unexpected structure falls back to upstream behaviour. Video selectors end with `/baldojnyi-photo`, which can match only such slides, so the whole carousel (mixed or photo-only) downloads in slide order with unique `[id]` filenames. Photo outputs (flag plus image extension allowlist) skip ffprobe and conversion. Audio mode passes `--extractor-args instagram:baldojnyi_photos=skip`: photo slides are dropped with warning `BALDOJNYI_PHOTOS_SKIPPED=N` (completed notice), and a photo-only post fails with `BALDOJNYI_PHOTO_ONLY` → error code `photo_only`, which offers no browser discovery. `scripts/instagram-photo-test.py` covers the plugin with a stub; `scripts/instagram-photo-integration-test.py` runs real yt-dlp (lazy and non-lazy extractors) with fixture posts when `YT_DLP_SOURCE` points to a checkout.
- Audio downloads keep the source first, then the app runs managed FFmpeg for MP3, M4A, Opus, or WAV with real percent, speed, and ETA. The source is preserved if conversion fails.
- Runtime assets stream directly to temporary files with a 30-second inactivity timeout and per-component byte caps, emit install progress, verify SHA-256 from disk, and expose per-component Retry without an app restart. Runtime maintenance is serialized both in-process and with a cross-process file lock, discovers updates without blocking the UI and installs sequentially behind a blocking cross-view modal, refuses to install during an active download, and keeps the previous executable available until its replacement succeeds. The application is single-instance on macOS and Windows; a second launch focuses the existing window. Success, attempted checks, and retry deadlines are stored separately; rate-limited failures back off across app restarts.
- Windows uses managed `yt-dlp.exe`, Deno, and `yt-dlp/FFmpeg-Builds` FFmpeg/FFprobe. Child commands use `CREATE_NO_WINDOW`; cancellation kills the complete Windows process tree; disk checks use the cross-platform filesystem API; cookie login defaults to Edge while the UI recommends Firefox when Chromium DPAPI/App-Bound Encryption prevents extraction; `.ru` route checks resolve the active Windows interface through `Find-NetRoute` and force PowerShell UTF-8 output so Cyrillic interface aliases survive. yt-dlp and FFmpeg pipe diagnostics remain readable after invalid legacy-codepage bytes, and persisted browser identifiers are allowlisted before reuse.
- A restrictive main-window Tauri CSP permits local scripts/styles, IPC and local assets; thumbnail images are cache-only. It does not allow remote images/scripts or `unsafe-eval`.
- Native scrollbar tracks are hidden across macOS and Windows while wheel, trackpad, keyboard, and programmatic scrolling remain enabled in every scrollable view and modal.

