## Release and Security Boundaries

- `.secrets/`, signing material, build output, runtime binaries, and release artifacts are ignored and must remain untracked.
- Versioning is mandatory and documented in `docs/VERSIONING_UK.md`: public releases use `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.HOTFIX`, with a one-digit `HOTFIX` from `0` to `9`. Tauri supports three-part SemVer, so its internal PATCH must equal `PUBLIC_PATCH * 10 + HOTFIX` (`0.5.1.1` → `0.5.11`, `0.5.2.0` → `0.5.20`). Fixes increment public PATCH, explicitly requested hotfixes increment HOTFIX, and user-visible features increment MINOR and reset public PATCH/HOTFIX. The release test enforces the mapping. Documentation/process-only changes wait for the next app release. Apply one bump per release based on its largest change, not one bump per commit.
- Every version bump must update and verify `package.json` (`version`, `releaseVersion`), root `package-lock.json` versions, `src-tauri/Cargo.toml`, the app entry in `src-tauri/Cargo.lock`, `src-tauri/tauri.conf.json`, `RELEASE_NOTES.md`, `.github/workflows/release.yml` (`workflow_dispatch.inputs.tag.default`), and current-release references in `docs/START_HERE_UK.md`, `docs/GITHUB_RELEASE_HANDOFF_UK.md`, and `READMEAI`. The release test enforces this consistency. Workflow dispatch copy uses the timeless example `vX.Y.Z.H`, while its release-specific default must always be `v<releaseVersion>` so the GitHub form opens prefilled.
- `.github/workflows/release.yml` is manual-only (`workflow_dispatch`), uses the protected `release` environment and standard GitHub-hosted runners, builds Windows x64 plus macOS arm64, and creates a draft release only after signing, notarization, stapling, and updater-signature checks.
- Every external GitHub Action is pinned to a full 40-character commit SHA. `.github/workflows/security.yml` runs npm audit, RustSec cargo-audit, and CodeQL on main/PRs plus a weekly schedule; Dependabot checks npm, Cargo, and Actions monthly in one non-major maintenance group. Enable GitHub's repository-level full-SHA enforcement immediately after this pinned workflow commit reaches `main`; enabling it earlier would intentionally block the still-tagged workflow on the old main branch.
- The protected release pipeline also generates and signs the runtime manifest with the existing updater private key, verifies and republishes the Windows FFmpeg archive, and refuses to assemble a draft unless the runtime manifest, signature, and pinned FFmpeg asset are present.
- Protected Apple/Tauri signing values are scoped to only the individual release steps that consume them; dependency installation and tests do not receive those secrets. Temporary Apple API key, certificate, and signing keychain material is removed immediately after the notarized build and before artifact verification or the external upload action. The runtime generator uses the read-only workflow token only for its GitHub API calls. Runtime manifest/signature responses are capped at 1 MiB before parsing or signature verification.
- `runtime-components.json` is shared through `/releases/latest`; schema version 1 must remain backward-compatible for every supported app release. A breaking schema requires a versioned endpoint and an app migration first. Clean-install runtime bootstrap can be smoke-tested only after publishing the draft because draft assets are intentionally absent from `/releases/latest`.
- The public four-part release version is stored as `package.json.releaseVersion`; Cargo/Tauri retain a monotonically increasing three-part SemVer because Tauri's updater parser requires SemVer. Public `0.8.0.0` was published on 2026-10-12. The prepared feature release is public `0.9.0.0`, internal `0.9.0`, and tag `v0.9.0.0`. Managed yt-dlp permanently follows the official nightly channel because upstream recommends it for regular users and stable can become stale as sites change. Each app release pins one exact nightly asset as the signed bootstrap. Successful startup maintenance then checks the official nightly release at most once per 24 hours, strictly validates its repository/tag/asset metadata, rejects version rollback, and completes after one API request when the accepted tag, cached verified SHA-256, current GitHub digest, and local file all agree. A new or changed nightly additionally requires GitHub's SHA-256 to equal upstream `SHA2-256SUMS`, bounds the exact binary transfer, hashes and version-probes the temporary binary once, and atomically replaces the working file without requiring a new app release. Metadata/checksum probes have bounded eight-second waits; binary transfer has a 30-second inactivity timeout and preserves the previous executable on failure. Official stable and nightly repositories remain narrowly allowlisted for signed-manifest schema compatibility. The lockfile must retain optional packages for every supported platform.
- Release notes are incremental: `RELEASE_NOTES.md` describes only the current release delta, starts with the exact current public-version heading, and may not repeat any published bullet archived in `docs/RELEASE_NOTES_ARCHIVE.md`. Immediately after publishing, append that release's exact notes to the archive before preparing another version. The release tests enforce the heading, version synchronization, and exact-bullet non-duplication.
- GitHub Release titles use `BaldojnyiDownloader <public-version>`; asset filenames keep their existing hyphenated platform-specific format.
- The combined `latest.json` contains `darwin-aarch64`, `windows-x86_64-nsis`, and compatibility alias `windows-x86_64`. The manifest generator fails closed if either platform or signature is missing.
- Public release assets always use human-readable names: `BaldojnyiDownloader-<public-version>-Mac-Apple-Silicon.dmg`, the matching signed `...-Mac-Apple-Silicon-AutoUpdate.app.tar.gz` updater archive, and `BaldojnyiDownloader-<public-version>-Windows-x64-Setup.exe`. Tauri's generated files are copied to these names without changing their bytes; `.sig` files follow the renamed updater artifact, and `latest.json` points only to the public names.
- Apple notarization variables are documented in `docs/SECRETS_UK.md`: `APPLE_API_ISSUER`, `APPLE_API_KEY` (Key ID), and `APPLE_API_KEY_PATH` pointing to a temporary `.p8` created from a protected secret and deleted during cleanup. Write collaborators may use repository-level Actions secrets while the protected `release` environment remains the approval gate; owner-managed environment secrets are an optional narrower scope.
- `scripts/build-macos.sh` is fail-closed: it accepts the updater key from a protected CI environment variable or ignored local file, requires a Developer ID Application identity plus either API-key or Keychain-profile notarization credentials, and verifies signature, hardened runtime, timestamp, DMG integrity, notarization, stapling, and Gatekeeper before generating release metadata.
- Release operation is documented in `docs/GITHUB_RELEASE_HANDOFF_UK.md`; the canonical secret names and runtime mapping remain in `docs/SECRETS_UK.md`.
- Before the first public release, the updater keypair was rotated on 2026-07-26 to a password-protected key. The matching private key and password are separate protected GitHub Actions secrets; the password is also stored in macOS Keychain under service `app.ytdlp.desktop.updater-signing`. Local `.secrets` permissions are owner-only. The pre-rotation pair remains only in the ignored owner-only `.secrets/archive` until this public-key PR is merged and verified.

